/* book Worked example: Q: An IGBT switches 10 A off a 400 V DC link at 20 kHz, with a duty ratio of at least 0.25. Its voltage rise must be limited to 500 V/µs. Design an R-C turn-off snubber: find Cs, the energy and power to be dissipated, and Rs if the discharge current at turn-on is to be at most 2 A. Check the discharge time.
   1. Cs = I / (dv/dt) = 10 A ÷ (500 × 106 V/s) = 2 × 10−8 F = 20 nF.
   2. Energy stored at turn-off: E = ½ Cs V² = 0.5 × 20 × 10−9 × 400² = 1.6 × 10−3 J = 1.6 mJ.
   3. Power in Rs: P = E fs = 1.6 mJ × 20 000 = 32 W, so use a resistor rated well above 32 W.
   4. Rs ≥ V / Imax = 400 V ÷ 2 A = 200 Ω.
   5. Discharge time ≈ 3 Rs Cs = 3 × 200 × 20 × 10−9 = 12 µs. The shortest on-time is 0.25 × 50 µs = 12.5 µs, so the capacitor just empties in time.
   Answer: Cs = 20 nF, Rs = 200 Ω, 1.6 mJ per cycle and 32 W in Rs; the 12 µs discharge fits inside the 12.5 µs minimum on-time. */
