/* book exam traps: A snubber does not save energy: the ½ Cs V² fs it takes from the switch is burnt in Rs (32 W in the worked example). | Cs without Rs dumps its charge into the switch at turn-on, limited only by stray resistance. | di/dt is a turn-on current limit fixed with a series inductor; dv/dt is an off-state voltage limit fixed with an R-C across the device. Do not swap the cures. | A fuse protects the device only if its clearing I²t is below the device's I²t rating, not because its current rating is lower. */
