/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - During a transition the switch has both a large voltage and a large current, and their product is power.
   - Esw = ½ V I tsw (inductive), Esw = V I tsw / 6 (resistive)
   - where Esw is the energy of one transition (J), V the blocking voltage (V), I the on-state current (A) and tsw the transition time (s).
   - Psw = ½ V I (ton + toff) fs
   - the average switching loss with an inductive load, in watts, where ton and toff are the turn-on and turn-off transition times (s) and fs the switching frequency (Hz).
   - Shorter transitions cut the loss; at fixed transition times, Psw is proportional to fs.
   - While on, a diode, thyristor, BJT or IGBT drops a roughly constant voltage, so Pcond = Von I D, where I is the current while conducting and D the fraction of the period it conducts.
   - the total heat the cooling path must remove, in watts.
   - Heat flows from the junction to the case, across the mounting interface to the heat sink, and from the sink to the ambient air.
   - Tj = Ta + P (RθJC + RθCS + RθSA)
*/
