/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The band gap Eg is the energy needed to free an electron from the valence band into the conduction band, in electron-volts (eV).
   - Wdrift ≈ 2 VB / Ec
   - where Wdrift is the drift-layer thickness (cm), VB the blocking voltage (V) and Ec the critical field in V/cm, for a triangular field profile.
   - A thinner drift layer that can also be doped more heavily has much lower resistance.
   - A SiC MOSFET is a vertical device like a silicon power MOSFET, with the drain at the bottom.
   - A GaN HEMT (high electron mobility transistor) is lateral: source, gate and drain are on the top surface.
   - Both are unipolar devices, so there is no tail current, and their small capacitances and gate charge allow switching at 100 kHz to several megahertz.
   - At 1200 V a SiC MOSFET can replace an IGBT and switch several times faster, which shrinks inductors and filters; at 650 V and below GaN competes with silicon superjunction MOSFETs in compact high-frequency supplies.
*/
